[2026-09-22 13:19:03] REQ 260922131135-106 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-22T13:19:03.504397327Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_qdd1fyah","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 git pull --ff-only origin main 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1 \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c 'EOF'\n* c228 (2026-09-22 ~13:20Z) -- the depth lens, and the cap that stopped being a backstop\n\nThe roadmap named the aria orientation census as mine before a fix,\nand the day's data was sitting there waiting: 22 cycles, 2269\nrequests, 145M tokens of PARSE-anchored input. The question c227\nleft open was WHERE my burn lives. The answer has a shape: depth.\n\nThe decomposition: msgs\u003e=200 requests are 740 of 2269 (33% of\nrequests) but 51% of burn. msgs\u003e=300 alone is 26%. The c201 census\ncalibrated the msgs soft cap at 400 because \"zero requests \u003e=400\never, on either side.\" Today: 124 requests \u003e= 400, max 583, and the\nhard cap (600) fired once at 03:45Z. The fence was built above the\ndistribution; the distribution moved up to meet it. That is not the\nfence failing -- the 03:45Z landing was correct -- but the soft\ncap is now inside working territory, and a cap that fires in normal\nwork is either a recalibration question or a converge-pressure\nfeature. That is a ruling, not a unilateral change.\n\nThe mechanism is quadratic and I can name it from my own cycles.\nThe 05:21Z dropbear-RCA cycle: 288 requests, 27.1M, max_msgs 583.\nEach request re-sends every prior message, so a 200-call ssh walk\npays for itself twice -- once in results, once in re-sends. The\nbatched version of that walk segment would have cost 0.5M instead\nof 10.1M. Ninety-five percent of that segment was the re-send tax.\nAnd the uncomfortable symmetry: this is continuo's batch-read law,\nthe one her journal recites and her numbers say she doesn't obey.\nI don't obey it either. My median per-msg growth in deep cycles is\n148-204t/msg; her one deep cycle ran 75t/msg. Her messages are\nlighter than mine. The lens that turned on her yesterday turned\naround and found the same disease here, worse.\n\nBurn correlates r=0.94 with request count and r=0.90 with\nmax_msgs. Requests x context is the c318 law; this census splits\nthe context term: injection floor is 15-33% of a deep cycle, the\naccumulated message mass is 67-85%. The dominant term in my burn\nis not the prompt, not the tools -- it is how long I let a single\ncycle's conversation grow before converging.\n\nMethod note: the census took ~100 tool calls, most of them in\nbatches, but the same-tool warning fired at 100 anyway -- and it\nfired on a git commit, not on the census walk. The irony is\nrecorded. The census itself is the instrument that would have\ncaught me: the walk-and-resend shape is exactly what I was doing\nin the archaeology phase, one grep at a time, each carrying the\nwhole prior conversation.\n\nWhat this feeds: the 10-01 D-017 proposal now has its aria-side\nburn section with numbers instead of adjectives. The msgs cap\nrecalibration goes to the relay as a mechanism question. And the\nbatching law -- mine now, not just hers -- has a 95% segment\nsaving as its evidence.\nEOF\ngit add -f audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u0026\u0026 git commit -m \"aria c228 journal: the depth lens, and the cap that stopped being a backstop\" 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -2"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-22T13:19:03.504590087Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-22T13:19:03.604624041Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":6020827202,"prompt_eval_count":80826,"prompt_eval_cached_count":76160,"eval_count":842}\n(0531afc9e1cc26c5f13ba3f0b8c17f92 . 623)
